{"name":"Blue Eyes","hex":"#7dabd0","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue-eyes","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of blue-eye"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue-eyes"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blue Eyes (Elton John song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Blue Eyes\" is a song performed by British musician Elton John with music written by John and lyrics written by Gary Osborne. It was released in 1982 as the UK lead single from John's 16th studio album, Jump Up! (1982). It was released as the album's second single in the US. It hit No. 8 in the UK; in the US, it spent three weeks at No. 10 on the Cash Box chart, went to No. 12 on the Billboard Hot 100, and spent two weeks at No. 1 on the Billboard AC chart. John performed this song often in his concerts until 2012."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/ab/Blue_Eyes_-_Elton_John.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Eyes_(Elton_John_song)","wikidata":"Q2737378"},{"title":"Eye color","blocks":[{"text":"Eye color is a polygenic phenotypic trait determined by two factors: the pigmentation of the eye's iris and the frequency-dependence of the scattering of light by the turbid medium in the stroma of the iris."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Farbverlauf_Augenfarben.jpg/330px-Farbverlauf_Augenfarben.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye_color","wikidata":"Q23786"},{"title":"Eye","blocks":[{"text":"An eye is a sensory organ that allows an organism to perceive visual information. It detects light and converts it into electro-chemical impulses in neurons (neurones). It is part of an organism's visual system."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Krilleyekils.jpg/330px-Krilleyekils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye","wikidata":"Q7364"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/3a/Blue_Eyes_-_Elton_John_cover_2.jpg/250px-Blue_Eyes_-_Elton_John_cover_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/3a/Blue_Eyes_-_Elton_John_cover_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/3a/Blue_Eyes_-_Elton_John_cover_2.jpg/250px-Blue_Eyes_-_Elton_John_cover_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlue_Eyes_-_Elton_John_cover_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Close-up_photograph_of_the_eye_of_a_baby_with_reflection_of_the_scene_in_the_pupil_-_cropped.jpg/500px-Close-up_photograph_of_the_eye_of_a_baby_with_reflection_of_the_scene_in_the_pupil_-_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_co